  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Edoardo Polli is an Italian textile industrialist who expanded his family's Manifattura Valle Brembana group through acquisitions. A grandson of its founder, he joined the business in 1974 and later took control of Legler. His expansion built a major cotton-textile group, but recession and debt subsequently brought restructuring and a division of the businesses.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Full regional business history documents family continuity, acquisitions and restructuring."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 54,
    "html": "<p>Edoardo Polli is an Italian textile industrialist who expanded his family's Manifattura Valle Brembana group through acquisitions. A grandson of its founder, he joined the business in 1974 and later took control of Legler. His expansion built a major cotton-textile group, but recession and debt subsequently brought restructuring and a division of the businesses.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Offshore racing gave textile manufacturer Edoardo Polli a second arena for building a team. He began competing with Stefano Casiraghi in 1986, then established a racing operation of his own. With experienced throttleman Franco Statua, he finished fourth overall at the 1987 world championships in Key West, winning the final race.\n\nThe following season, his team adopted the Rainbow name and added a Cougar catamaran. Polli won races at Nice, Saint-Tropez, Poole and Cowes. His involvement extended beyond driving: he directed a team that brought together pilots, navigators and engine specialists, treating technical development as part of the competition.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 89,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary team brochure gives debut, Key West result, subsequent wins and directed team structure. Captain's independent recollection corroborates Saint-Tropez participation."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S3",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 99,
    "html": "<p>Offshore racing gave textile manufacturer Edoardo Polli a second arena for building a team. He began competing with Stefano Casiraghi in 1986, then established a racing operation of his own. With experienced throttleman Franco Statua, he finished fourth overall at the 1987 world championships in Key West, winning the final race.</p>\r\n<p>The following season, his team adopted the Rainbow name and added a Cougar catamaran. Polli won races at Nice, Saint-Tropez, Poole and Cowes. His involvement extended beyond driving: he directed a team that brought together pilots, navigators and engine specialists, treating technical development as part of the competition.</p>\r\n"
  },
